// The world a tau GATEWAY TRANSFORMER is compiled to.
//
// # Functions traffic passes through, in the order they run
//
// A gateway or a proxy declares `transformers:` — a LIST, because the order
// they run in is what they mean — and each entry says what happens if it fails:
// `closed` rejects the traffic, `open` passes it through untransformed. That
// choice is genuinely per use, and it is dangerous in both directions: a filter
// that fails open stops filtering exactly when something is wrong, and an
// enrichment that fails closed takes the site down to add a header.
//
// # Why this is not a handler with a convention
//
// A handler answers a request. A transformer answers with EITHER a request that
// carries on or a response that stops it, and those are different things: the
// first has a method and a path and goes to the next transformer, the second
// has a status and goes back to the client. Encoding "carry on" as a response
// with a magic status would make every real status ambiguous — a transformer
// that legitimately wants to return 200 could not.
//
// # It runs on every request, which is the whole difference from the others
//
// An authorizer and an instance validator are asked once and their answer is
// kept, so instantiating one per question costs a cold start that amortises
// away. A transformer runs on EVERY request and nothing about its answer can be
// kept — it is a function of the request. Measured on this tree, instantiating a
// small component is ~13ms, which is not a thing to add to every request at the
// edge, so transformer instances are POOLED exactly like handlers: the pool's
// warm slots, cold-start deduplication, per-spec concurrency and scale-to-zero
// are engine-agnostic and none of it is specific to answering a request.
package taubyte:gateway-transformer@0.1.0;

world gateway-transformer {
    // THE SAME SUBSET THE OTHER DECIDING WORLDS GET: state, and nothing that
    // sends. A transformer that publishes to a queue makes a request's journey
    // through the chain unrepeatable, and a chain is retried.
    import taubyte:sql/store@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:kv/store@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:document/store@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:vector/store@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:cache/store@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:secret/vault@0.1.0;
    import taubyte:workload/identity@0.1.0;

    // THE SAME `request` AND `response` THE HANDLER WORLD DECLARES, field for
    // field, because this is the same traffic — and inline here for the reason
    // handler.wit gives: a `use` from an imported interface leaves the export's
    // signature naming a type the host must resolve separately.
    record header {
        name: string,
        value: string,
    }

    record request {
        method: string,
        path: string,
        headers: list<header>,
        body: list<u8>,
    }

    record response {
        status: u16,
        headers: list<header>,
        body: list<u8>,
    }

    // What a transformer decided.
    //
    // `forward` carries the request on to the next transformer and then to
    // whatever the route names — changed or not, since a transformer that
    // decided to change nothing returns what it was given. `answer` stops the
    // chain here and the response goes back to the client, which is how a
    // filter rejects without the request ever reaching a workload.
    variant outcome {
        forward(request),
        answer(response),
    }

    export transform: func(req: request) -> outcome;
}
